.Dd @MDOCDATE@
.Dt CLOWN-CAPABILITY-PROFILE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm clown-capability-profile
.Nd check a capability profile against the RFC 0019 ruleset
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm clown capability-profile check
.Op Fl -patch
.Ar file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm clown capability-profile check
runs the RFC 0019 ruleset over
.Ar file
and prints every finding, one per line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
severity node: message
.Ed
where
.Ar severity
is
.Sy refuse
or
.Sy warn ,
.Ar node
is the path of ids the finding is about
.Pq Ql - No for a head or whole-document finding ,
and
.Ar message
begins with the rule's id
.Pq e.g.\& Ql R-4: ,
followed by what to write instead.
A clean profile prints nothing.
.Pp
The rules go beyond what ajour's parser accepts: a profile that parses may
still be one no one should bless.
This verb is the advisory, authoring side; whatever blesses a profile runs the
same rules and refuses on a
.Sy refuse
finding, showing
.Sy warn
findings at the prompt.
.Pp
.Ar file
is a capability profile: a hyphence head over a stencil body.
The head's type line is
.Ql ! espalier-profile-v1
and it carries
.Sy role ,
.Sy harness
and
.Sy mode .
A file that does not open with a
.Ql ---
boundary is a head-less body, checked alone: the head rules
.Pq Sy H-*
and the mode rules
.Pq Sy M-*
do not run on it.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -patch
Check
.Ar file
as an act's body, a patch, instead of a profile: every leaf must carry
.Sy deny ,
and only the rules RFC 0019 marks for patches run.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Sy 0
when no finding refuses (warnings may be printed),
.Sy 1
when at least one does, and
.Sy 2
on a usage error or a file that cannot be read.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr clown 1 ,
.Xr ajour 7 ,
.Xr clown-catalog 7
.Sh STANDARDS
RFC 0019 (the capability-profile ruleset) and RFC 0018 (the capability catalog
contract whose vocabulary the rules read).
